  <p class="secondary">Part VIII</p>
  <h1>Excitations and Spectra</h1>
  <p class="lede">A ground-state calculation asks which state minimizes the energy. Spectroscopy asks what happens when energy or a particle is exchanged with the system. The answer therefore depends on which many-body state is created, whether the electron number changes, and how the external probe couples to that state. Those questions lead beyond a ground-state Kohn–Sham spectrum to charged quasiparticles, neutral electron–hole excitations, and probe-dependent spectral weight.</p>

  <h2>A spectrum starts by naming what the experiment changes</h2>
  <p>Parts IV–VI built a ground-state route: a density determines an auxiliary Kohn–Sham problem, and its self-consistent solution supplies a ground-state energy and density from which specified derivatives can be taken. Part III already explained why any periodic one-particle Hamiltonian—including a periodic Kohn–Sham Hamiltonian—has Bloch states and bands. None of those statements says that every Kohn–Sham eigenvalue is an exact energy for adding, removing, or optically exciting a real electron.</p>
  <p>The missing distinction is easiest to see through particle number. Let <math class="math-inline" aria-label="E zero superscript N"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_0^N</annotation></semantics></math> be the ground-state energy of the interacting system with <math class="math-inline" aria-label="N"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> electrons. A removal experiment leaves an <math class="math-inline" aria-label="N minus one"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N-1</annotation></semantics></math>-electron system; an addition experiment creates an <math class="math-inline" aria-label="N plus one"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N+1</annotation></semantics></math>-electron system. Optical absorption normally leaves the total electron number at <math class="math-inline" aria-label="N"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> while promoting the system to another many-body state.</p>

  <figure class="excitation-sectors" aria-labelledby="excitation-sectors-caption">
    <div class="atlas-panel-grid atlas-panel-grid--3">
      <section>
        <h3>Ground state</h3>
        <p><math class="math-inline" aria-label="N electrons"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mtext> electrons</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\ \text&#123;electrons&#125;</annotation></semantics></math></p>
        <span>Which state minimizes the energy for the declared system?</span>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h3>Charged excitation</h3>
        <p><math class="math-inline" aria-label="N plus or minus one electrons"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext> electrons</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\!\pm\!1\ \text&#123;electrons&#125;</annotation></semantics></math></p>
        <span>What energy and amplitude accompany addition or removal?</span>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h3>Neutral excitation</h3>
        <p><math class="math-inline" aria-label="N electrons in an excited state"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mtext> electrons, excited state</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\ \text&#123;electrons, excited state&#125;</annotation></semantics></math></p>
        <span>Which fixed-particle-number state is created, and can the probe reach it?</span>
      </section>
    </div>
    <figcaption id="excitation-sectors-caption">The three questions refer to different many-body sectors. A single list of one-particle eigenvalues cannot be assumed to answer all three.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>The fundamental gap is a charged total-energy difference</h2>
  <p>Before introducing any quasiparticle approximation, we can define the energy required to change particle number. The ionization energy <math class="math-inline" aria-label="I"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> compares the <math class="math-inline" aria-label="N"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and <math class="math-inline" aria-label="N minus one"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N-1</annotation></semantics></math> ground states; the electron affinity <math class="math-inline" aria-label="A"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> compares the <math class="math-inline" aria-label="N"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and <math class="math-inline" aria-label="N plus one"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N+1</annotation></semantics></math> ground states. Their difference is the fundamental charged gap:</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="The ionization energy is the N minus one ground-state energy minus the N-electron ground-state energy, the electron affinity is the N-electron energy minus the N plus one energy, and the fundamental gap is I minus A">
      <semantics>
        <mtable columnalign="right center left" columnspacing="0.5em" rowspacing="0.75em">
          <mtr><mtd><mi>I</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr>
          <mtr><mtd><mi>A</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr>
          <mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">g</mi><mtext>fund</mtext></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr>
        </mtable>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I=E_0^&#123;N-1&#125;-E_0^N,\qquad A=E_0^N-E_0^&#123;N+1&#125;,\qquad E_&#123;\mathrm&#123;g&#125;&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;fund&#125;&#125;=I-A=E_0^&#123;N+1&#125;+E_0^&#123;N-1&#125;-2E_0^N.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Read the last line as an energy cost, not as a difference between two arbitrary orbital labels. One process removes an electron and lets the other electrons rearrange while the external potential and nuclear geometry remain fixed; the other adds an electron under those same fixed external conditions. The gap therefore compares interacting many-body energies of one electronic Hamiltonian in different particle-number sectors. Allowing the nuclei or structure to relax would instead define an adiabatic charging or charge-transition energy.</p>
  <p>For an extended solid these differences are understood in the appropriate thermodynamic and electrostatic limit. Charged periodic cells introduce boundary and finite-size questions that a bare formula does not solve. The conceptual conclusion is nevertheless stable: the fundamental gap is defined by addition and removal, whereas a Kohn–Sham gap is a difference between eigenvalues of an auxiliary ground-state Hamiltonian. Exact ground-state DFT relates the two only with additional structure, including the exchange–correlation derivative discontinuity; an approximate Kohn–Sham eigenvalue gap is not the definition above.</p>
  <p class="equation-source">Sources: Cohen and Louie, <cite>Fundamentals of Condensed Matter Physics</cite>, Chs. 7 and 13; Onida, Reining, and Rubio, <cite>Reviews of Modern Physics</cite> 74, 601 (2002), Secs. I.B and III.A.</p>

  <h2>The one-particle Green function organizes addition and removal</h2>
  <p>A charged excitation is still associated with adding or removing one electron, but the other electrons respond. The resulting state is not generally “one Kohn–Sham orbital occupied or emptied.” The one-particle Green function is designed for this question: it records the amplitude for an electron added at one space–time point to propagate to another, together with the corresponding hole process. Its poles occur at interacting addition and removal energies, while its residues tell how strongly a simple one-particle operation overlaps each many-body state.</p>
  <p>If the electrons were independent, each orbital would contribute one sharp pole with unit one-particle weight. Interaction changes both facts. A dominant pole may remain, but some weight can move into satellites involving additional excitations, and a state embedded in a decay continuum can acquire a finite linewidth. A quasiparticle is the useful case in which one dressed pole retains enough weight and lifetime to support a particle-like label.</p>

  <figure class="core-diagram spectral-weight" aria-labelledby="spectral-weight-title spectral-weight-description spectral-weight-caption">
